(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["reflexes"]="<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-a-cardiovascular-reflex\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What is a cardiovascular reflex?</h2>\n<p>Neural and Hormonal Control described the <strong>controls</strong>: the <span data-term=\"vasomotor-centre\">vasomotor centre</span>, the sympathetic nerves, the vagus. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control</a>)</em> This chapter describes the <strong>sensors</strong> that tell the centre when to act.</p>\n<p>Every reflex has the same five links:</p>\n<p><strong>Stimulus → receptor → sensory (afferent) nerve → medulla → autonomic (efferent) nerves → heart and vessels</strong></p>\n<p>Most cardiovascular reflexes are <strong>negative feedback</strong>: they push the change back the other way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-baroreceptors-the-body-s-pressure-sensors\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Baroreceptors: the body's pressure sensors</h2>\n<p><strong>Baroreceptors</strong> are stretch-sensitive nerve endings in the walls of the large arteries. They don't sense pressure directly: they sense <strong>stretch</strong>. Higher pressure stretches the wall more, so they <strong>fire faster</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Site</th>\n  <th>Sensory nerve</th>\n  <th>Arrives at</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Carotid sinus</strong> (where the common carotid divides)</td>\n  <td><strong>Carotid sinus nerve</strong>, a branch of the <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)</strong></td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"nts\">NTS</span></strong> in the medulla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aortic arch</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Vagus nerve (X)</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>NTS</strong> in the medulla</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both sit in <strong>high-pressure</strong> arteries, right at the &quot;inlets&quot; to the brain and body.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> The baroreflex, step by step</h2>\n<div class=\"view-switch\">\n<figure class=\"learning-figure baroreflex-figure static-view\" id=\"cvs18-baroreflex\"><figcaption>The baroreflex loop when blood pressure rises</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 470\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Loop diagram. Blood pressure rises, so the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors fire faster. Signals travel in cranial nerves IX and X to the NTS in the medulla. The NTS excites the depressor area, which inhibits the pressor area, so sympathetic firing to the heart, arterioles and veins falls. The NTS also excites the vagal neurons, so vagal firing to the SA node rises. The heart becomes slower and weaker, arterioles dilate and veins relax, so blood pressure falls back towards normal. When pressure falls, every arrow reverses.\"><defs><marker id=\"bx-head\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"bx-arrowhead\"/></marker></defs><g class=\"bx-arrow\"><path d=\"M280 32H310V82\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M310 142V170\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M230 198H107V254\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M310 224V254\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M107 314V344\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M300 314V344\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M107 404V426\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M300 404V426\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/><path d=\"M24 445H10V32H118\" marker-end=\"url(#bx-head)\"/></g><g class=\"bx-node\"><rect x=\"120\" y=\"8\" width=\"160\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"84\" width=\"160\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"172\" width=\"160\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"24\" y=\"256\" width=\"166\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"256\" width=\"180\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"24\" y=\"346\" width=\"166\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"346\" width=\"180\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\"/></g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"428\" width=\"366\" height=\"34\" rx=\"10\" class=\"bx-effect\"/><g text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" class=\"bx-lab\">Blood pressure</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" class=\"bx-sub\"><tspan class=\"bx-up\">rises ↑</tspan></text><text x=\"310\" y=\"104\" class=\"bx-lab\">Baroreceptors</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"120\" class=\"bx-sub\">carotid sinus, aorta</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"136\" class=\"bx-sub\"><tspan class=\"bx-up\">fire faster ↑</tspan></text><text x=\"310\" y=\"194\" class=\"bx-lab\">IX and X → NTS</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"212\" class=\"bx-sub\">in the medulla</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"278\" class=\"bx-lab\">Depressor area <tspan class=\"bx-up\">↑</tspan></text><text x=\"107\" y=\"298\" class=\"bx-sub\">inhibits pressor area</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"278\" class=\"bx-lab\">Vagal neurons <tspan class=\"bx-up\">↑</tspan></text><text x=\"300\" y=\"298\" class=\"bx-sub\">in the medulla</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"368\" class=\"bx-lab\">Sympathetic <tspan class=\"bx-down\">↓</tspan></text><text x=\"107\" y=\"388\" class=\"bx-sub\">heart, arterioles, veins</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"368\" class=\"bx-lab\">Vagus to SA node <tspan class=\"bx-up\">↑</tspan></text><text x=\"300\" y=\"388\" class=\"bx-sub\">slows the heart</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"450\" class=\"bx-sub\">Slower, weaker heart · arterioles dilate · veins relax</text></g><text x=\"18\" y=\"76\" class=\"bx-lab bx-down\">…so pressure</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"94\" class=\"bx-lab bx-down\">falls back ↓</text></svg><p>When blood pressure <strong>falls</strong>, every arrow reverses: the baroreceptors fire less, sympathetic firing rises, vagal firing falls, and the heart speeds up while the vessels constrict. </p></figure>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"reflex-tracer\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Reflex tracer</figcaption><p>Three buttons: \"Blood pressure rises\", \"Blood pressure falls\" and \"Intracranial pressure rises\". Each press steps through the reflex one link at a time (Next / Back), lighting up each part of the loop with an up or down arrow and one sentence of explanation. A final summary row shows heart rate, contractility, arteriolar tone, venous tone and blood pressure. Shown with the static baroreflex figure behind the Diagram | Interactive switch.</p></figure>\n</div>\n\n<p><strong>When blood pressure rises:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The arterial walls <strong>stretch more</strong>, so the <span data-term=\"baroreceptors\">baroreceptors</span> <strong>fire faster</strong>.</li>\n<li>Signals travel up IX and X to the <strong><span data-term=\"nts\">NTS</span></strong>.</li>\n<li>The NTS <strong>excites the depressor area</strong>, which <strong>inhibits the pressor area</strong>. Sympathetic firing <strong>falls</strong>.</li>\n<li>The NTS also <strong>excites the vagal neurons</strong>. Vagal firing to the heart <strong>rises</strong>.</li>\n<li>Result: the heart beats <strong>slower</strong> and <strong>less forcefully</strong>, arterioles <strong>dilate</strong> and veins <strong>relax</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cardiac output and resistance both fall, so blood pressure <strong>falls back towards normal</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>When blood pressure falls</strong>, everything runs in reverse:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Less stretch, so the baroreceptors <strong>fire less</strong>.</li>\n<li>The depressor area is <strong>no longer excited</strong>, so the pressor area is <strong>released</strong>. Sympathetic firing <strong>rises</strong>.</li>\n<li>Vagal firing <strong>falls</strong>.</li>\n<li>Result: <strong>faster</strong> heart (<strong>tachycardia</strong>), <strong>stronger</strong> beats, arterioles <strong>constrict</strong>, veins <strong>squeeze</strong> stored blood towards the heart.</li>\n<li>Blood pressure <strong>rises back towards normal</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What the baroreflex is good at, and what it isn't:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is excellent at buffering <strong>sudden</strong> changes: standing up, a sudden bleed. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/gravity\">→ Gravity</a>)</em></li>\n<li>It is <strong>not</strong> in charge of long-term blood pressure. If pressure stays high for days, the baroreceptors <strong>reset</strong> to the new level and defend that instead. Long-term pressure is set mainly by the kidneys' control of salt and water. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral?section=6-fast-slow-and-slower-putting-it-together\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control, §6</a>)</em></li>\n<li>It <strong>can't replace lost blood</strong>. A fast heart after a bleed shows the reflex is working, not that the problem is solved. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which nerve carries signals from the carotid sinus baroreceptors?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The vagus nerve (X), via its branches to the aortic arch</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The hypoglossal nerve (XII), via its branches in the neck</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), via the carotid sinus nerve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The sympathetic chain, via the superior cervical ganglion</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:e1e117c5\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Carotid sinus → IX. Aortic arch → X.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient's blood pressure suddenly rises. What does the baroreflex do to heart rate and arterioles?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Slows the heart and dilates the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Speeds the heart and constricts the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slows the heart and constricts the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Speeds the heart and dilates the arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:1a1763b7\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> More stretch → more vagal firing and less sympathetic firing: a slower heart and wider arterioles bring pressure down.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can't the baroreflex alone explain long-term high blood pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The baroreceptors stop firing completely after a few hours</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The baroreceptors reset to defend whatever pressure persists</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The baroreceptors only respond to falls in blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The baroreceptors sense oxygen, not the stretch of the artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:549ffc75\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> After days at a higher pressure, the reflex defends the new level. The kidneys set long-term pressure.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Low-pressure receptors: sensing how full the circulation is</h2>\n<p>There are also stretch receptors in <strong>low-pressure</strong> sites: the walls of the <strong>atria</strong> (where the venae cavae and pulmonary veins enter) and the <strong>pulmonary vessels</strong>. Because pressure there depends mainly on how much blood fills them, they act as <strong>volume receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes two types in the atria:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Type A</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Type B</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Fires during</td>\n  <td><strong>Atrial systole</strong> (contraction)</td>\n  <td>Later, during <strong>atrial filling</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mainly signals</td>\n  <td>Heart rate</td>\n  <td>How <strong>full</strong> the atria are</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>When the atria are <strong>overfilled</strong> (too much volume), the type B receptors fire more. This triggers responses that <strong>get rid of volume</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vasodilation</strong> and a <strong>fall in blood pressure</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Less <span data-term=\"adh\">ADH</span></strong> is released, so the kidneys <strong>excrete more water</strong> (the lecture also notes less ACTH).</li>\n<li>The stretched atria release <strong><span data-term=\"anp\">ANP</span></strong>, which does the same. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral?section=5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control, §5</a>)</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>rise</strong> in heart rate (the <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong>), which pumps the extra blood onwards, out of the congested veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Notice the surprise:</strong> this reflex lowers pressure but <strong>speeds</strong> the heart. That is one of the lecture’s “exceptions” (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions\">§7</a>).</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> In humans, the Bainbridge reflex is weak and usually outweighed by the arterial baroreflex; whether the heart rate rises or falls with extra volume depends on the balance between the two.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-chemoreceptors\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Chemoreceptors</h2>\n<p><strong>Peripheral chemoreceptors</strong> sit in small clusters of cells, the <strong>carotid bodies</strong> and <strong>aortic bodies</strong>, close to the baroreceptors. They sense <strong>low O₂</strong>, <strong>high CO₂</strong> and <strong>high H⁺</strong> in arterial blood. Their main job is to <strong>stimulate breathing</strong>. They also raise sympathetic drive to the vessels, which helps hold up blood pressure when it is very low, because low flow starves the chemoreceptor cells of oxygen.</p>\n<p><strong>Don't mix them up:</strong> the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> (baroreceptor) senses <strong>stretch</strong>; the <strong>carotid body</strong> (chemoreceptor) senses <strong>blood chemistry</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The vasomotor centre also senses chemistry directly.</strong> If its own blood supply falls, the local <strong>hypoxia and high CO₂</strong> stimulate it, and sympathetic firing rises. That is the basis of the Cushing reflex (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed\">§6</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> The Cushing reflex: when the brain is squeezed</h2>\n<p>When <strong><span data-term=\"icp\">intracranial pressure</span></strong> rises (from a tumour, a blood clot or <span data-term=\"hydrocephalus\">hydrocephalus</span>), it compresses the brain's vessels and <strong><span data-term=\"cpp\">cerebral perfusion pressure</span> falls</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\">→ Cerebral Circulation, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Blood flow to the <strong>medulla</strong> falls, so the vasomotor centre becomes <strong>hypoxic</strong> and <strong>hypercapnic</strong>.</li>\n<li>That <strong>directly stimulates</strong> the vasomotor centre: sympathetic firing surges, and <strong>arterial pressure rises</strong> sharply, forcing blood back into the brain.</li>\n<li>The arterial <strong>baroreceptors</strong> detect the high pressure and trigger <strong>reflex bradycardia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pressure on the brainstem also disturbs breathing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Cushing triad: hypertension + bradycardia + irregular breathing.</strong> It is a <strong>late, dangerous sign</strong> that the brain is being compressed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Other inputs, and the lecture’s “exceptions”</h2>\n<p>The vasomotor centre doesn't only listen to pressure and chemical sensors. The lecture lists other influences:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Input</th>\n  <th>Effect on blood pressure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anger, excitement</strong> (cortex → hypothalamus)</td>\n  <td><strong>Rises</strong>, with a faster heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Sharp, brief pain</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Rises</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Prolonged, severe pain</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Falls</strong>: vasodilation, and possibly <strong>fainting</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fear and grief</strong> (cortex)</td>\n  <td><strong>Falls</strong>, with a slower heart: the basis of an emotional faint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Lung inflation</strong> (vagal sensory fibres)</td>\n  <td><strong>Falls</strong>: vasodilation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Hypoxia, high CO₂</strong> (direct, on the vasomotor centre)</td>\n  <td><strong>Rises</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Usually, heart rate and blood pressure move in the same direction</strong> in these responses. But a reflex that is <strong>correcting</strong> a pressure change moves heart rate in the <strong>opposite</strong> direction. Always start with the <strong>stimulus</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Stimulus</th>\n  <th>Blood pressure</th>\n  <th>Heart rate</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Haemorrhage</strong></td>\n  <td>Low</td>\n  <td><strong>Fast</strong></td>\n  <td>Baroreflex: less stretch → more sympathetic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Raised <span data-term=\"icp\">ICP</span> (Cushing)</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>High</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Slow</strong></td>\n  <td>Brainstem ischaemia raises pressure; baroreflex slows the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Overfilled atria (Bainbridge)</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Lower</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Fast</strong></td>\n  <td><span data-term=\"volume-receptors\">Volume receptors</span>: dilate and pump the excess onwards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–7\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–7 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How does the body respond when the atria are overstretched by too much volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. More ADH and less ANP, so the kidneys keep salt and water</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. More aldosterone, so the kidneys keep sodium and water</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. More renin, so angiotensin II constricts the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Less ADH and more ANP, so the kidneys lose salt and water</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:1873ecf5\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Atrial stretch signals overfilling, so the responses all reduce volume.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What is the difference between the carotid sinus and the carotid body?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The sinus senses O₂, CO₂ and H⁺; the body senses arterial stretch</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The sinus senses arterial stretch; the body senses O₂, CO₂ and H⁺</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both sense stretch, but the sinus responds only to falls in pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Both sense chemistry, but the body sends signals through the vagus</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:5f853c37\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Sinus = baroreceptor. Body = chemoreceptor. Both send signals through IX.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with a head injury develops hypertension and a heart rate of 45. What explains the slow heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The injured brain directly stimulates the SA node to fire more slowly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low intracranial pressure removes the sympathetic drive to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Atrial stretch receptors respond to the rise in blood volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Baroreceptors respond to the high pressure caused by brainstem ischaemia</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"reflexes:c5453c78\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Rising ICP makes the vasomotor centre ischaemic, which raises arterial pressure. The baroreflex then slows the heart: the Cushing reflex.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reflex:</strong> stimulus → receptor → afferent nerve → medulla (NTS) → autonomic nerves → heart and vessels. Mostly negative feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Baroreceptors</strong> sense <strong>stretch</strong>: carotid sinus (IX) and aortic arch (X).</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure rises</strong> → more firing → more vagal, less sympathetic → slower heart, dilation → pressure falls. <strong>Pressure falls</strong> → the reverse: <strong>tachycardia</strong> and constriction.</li>\n<li>The baroreflex buffers <strong>sudden</strong> changes; it <strong>resets</strong> with sustained changes and can't replace lost volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial volume receptors</strong> (type A in atrial systole, type B in filling): overfilling → dilation, less ADH, more ANP, faster heart (<strong>Bainbridge</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Chemoreceptors</strong> (carotid and aortic bodies) sense low O₂, high CO₂ and H⁺; mainly drive breathing.</li>\n<li><strong>Cushing reflex:</strong> raised ICP → medullary ischaemia → hypertension → baroreflex bradycardia; irregular breathing. A late sign.</li>\n<li><strong>Other inputs:</strong> anger and sharp pain raise pressure; fear, grief, prolonged pain and lung inflation lower it.</li>\n<li><strong>Start with the stimulus:</strong> haemorrhage = low BP + fast heart; Cushing = high BP + slow heart; Bainbridge = lower BP + fast heart.</li>\n</ol>\n";
